<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neo-Hittite states</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neo-Hittite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neo-Hittite states were city-states in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, such as Carchemish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a>, that emerged after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> around 1200 BCE. They preserved elements of Hittite political and artistic tradition, including hieroglyphic Luwian inscriptions. They were absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> in the eighth century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neo-hittite-states.jpg" alt="Neo-Hittite states" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rowanwindwhistler; derivative work by Morningstar1814 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neohititas-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
